{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"989eeed1260d6fd50d93a92adc0bd0d2734ee66e7258643143f1cc853a20a78c","slug":"pages.p141-m11-rules-for-means-and-variances-of-random-v","location":"/pages/p141-m11-rules-for-means-and-variances-of-random-variables-1-of.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Shifting and Scaling a Random Variable","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"City College of San Francisco","given":"City College","non_dropping_particle":"of","family":"San Francisco"},"name":"City College of San Francisco","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","CC":true}},"abbreviations":{"ANOVA":"Analysis of Variance","CI":"Confidence Interval","CLT":"Central Limit Theorem","EDA":"Exploratory Data Analysis","IQR":"Interquartile Range","SD":"Standard Deviation","SE":"Standard Error","SRS":"Simple Random Sample","INCREASE":null,"INCREASED":null,"DECREASES":null,"OBSERVED":null,"ROSE":null},"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"thumbnail":"/textbook/build/m11-shift-hist-fb8d4d5d44403b3e67168d79c24c1986.svg","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"p141-m11-rules-for-means-and-variances-of-random-variables-1-of.md","url":"/textbook/build/p141-m11-rules-for-m-c5a3d2765d02de5f82c1c65c29efd6fa.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rules for Means and Variances of Random Variables","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"xXPz6kTEJX"}],"identifier":"rules-for-means-and-variances-of-random-variables","label":"Rules for Means and Variances of Random Variables","html_id":"rules-for-means-and-variances-of-random-variables","implicit":true,"key":"c0jqopscQc"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"So far we’ve learned how to calculate the mean and standard deviation of a random variable, and how to interpret these numerical measures. Let’s look at a motivating example that will show us what kinds of situations we may find these rules useful in.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"FRLRAswvq7"}],"key":"WbEXHaSsRs"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Xavier’s Production Line","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"C5cpTkrlwJ"}],"key":"VO6FGAzxCu"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assume that operating Xavier’s production line costs $50 per hour, and that the repair cost of one defective part is $5. If X is the number of defective parts produced per hour, then the hourly cost would be ","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"UbFPWsrya8"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"50 + 5X","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"a7W9gQ8i4w"}],"key":"anzBPlfp8K"},{"type":"text","value":". Note that since X is a random variable, so is the cost, 50 + 5X, and we might be interested in the mean and standard deviation of the hourly cost of operation for Xavier’s production line.","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"OuIXTe8FmP"}],"key":"nQXWGvzFfF"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"If we know the mean and standard deviation for the number of defective parts (X), is there an easy way to find the mean and standard deviation for the hourly cost (50 + 5X)?","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"DpccX8V4Ue"}],"key":"atLnsBblQD"}],"class":"tip","key":"fD7k9Rzxb6"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In General","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"hpP6QfPk0A"}],"identifier":"in-general","label":"In General","html_id":"in-general","implicit":true,"key":"Y3chRvSqoI"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sometimes a new random variable of interest arises when we take an existing random variable and multiply by a constant and/or add a constant to its values. In the example above, we both multiplied X by 5 and added 50. We will return to the above example after exploring how such changes affect the center (mean) and spread (standard deviation) of random variables in general.","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"YQkOEYNv1a"}],"key":"GZUFV9lhHs"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consider the random variable X with the probability distribution P(X = ","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"AAwdyLTWr9"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"-2) = 0.1","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2) = 0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;